{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794551076408,"sessionID":"ses_e2c7999e1ffeYcD6BDSYeMhQPJ","part":{"id":"prt_1d3945638001eqbySsKZPTtAow","sessionID":"ses_e2c7999e1ffeYcD6BDSYeMhQPJ","messageID":"msg_1d3944331001flAA9ZShMmSQBB","type":"step-start","snapshot":"807879471a356c10897747f9356711d621187fbd"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794551085642,"sessionID":"ses_e2c7999e1ffeYcD6BDSYeMhQPJ","part":{"partID":"prt_1d39477c5002OtL2osVVmXZmPN","sessionID":"ses_e2c7999e1ffeYcD6BDSYeMhQPJ","messageID":"msg_1d3944331001flAA9ZShMmSQBB","type":"tool","id":"call_25f77af28f6449aa8808f896","tool":"shell","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research; grep -n \"attach\\|command row\\|slash command\\|MODE_COMMANDS\\|policy\\|surface\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/SKILL.md .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/README.md .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/ARCHITECTURE.md | head -40"},"output":".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/SKILL.md:170:- [`references/runtime/legacy-tool-bridge.md`](references/runtime/legacy-tool-bridge.md): historical bridge policy record.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/SKILL.md:173:- [`references/config/db-path-policy.md`](references/config/db-path-policy.md): package-local SQLite path policy.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/SKILL.md:220:- `sk-code` owns implementation once routing selects a code surface.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/README.md:42:system-skill-advisor is the standalone Gate 2 routing surface for Spec Kit. It runs as a resident daemon behind one CLI front door, so routing can be tuned and rolled back without touching memory or code-graph systems. It fuses five scoring lanes into one calibrated recommendation, returns per-lane attribution without leaking prompt content and surfaces a trust state on every response so the caller knows whether to use the result or treat it as degraded.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/README.md:44:The single front door is the daemon-backed CLI at `.skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs`, which speaks the advisor's own newline-delimited protocol over a unix socket. When the daemon is unreachable, the CLI answers from the Python scorer at `runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py` and marks the response degraded, so the prompt-time brief reports `Advisor: stale` rather than claiming live. The skill also owns the documentation for the OpenCode plugin surface and the `/goal` plugin.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/README.md:103:- Constant advisor policy is delivered in full on the first proven message and after registered lifecycle boundaries.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/README.md:135:`advisor_recommend` accepts eight options: `topK` sets how many candidates to return (1 to 10), `includeAttribution` adds per-lane score breakdowns, `includeAbstainReasons` surfaces why lower-ranked candidates were not selected, `confidenceThreshold` overrides the minimum surfaced confidence, `uncertaintyThreshold` overrides the maximum surfaced uncertainty and `includeCompiledRoute` is an optional boolean that defaults to true. Passing `false` skips the compiled-route enrichment so no compiled-route subprocess starts. The prompt hooks send `true`, because a recommendation's `command` comes from its compiled route. `compiledRouteTopOnly` is an optional boolean that defaults to false. Passing `true` looks up the compiled route for the first recommendation only, which saves one subprocess for each further hub. The prompt hooks send it as `true`, so only the top pick can carry a `command`. `surfaceHint` forwards a code surface the caller already knows, such as `SKILLED` (1 to 64 characters), to the compiled-route front door, which ignores an unknown one. `advisor_validate` requires `confirmHeavyRun: true` because it executes the full validation bundle (corpus, holdout, parity, safety, latency).\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/README.md:176:Command rows use the same embedder. The bridge deriver writes an `embeddingText` for each of the 45 command rows the TypeScript scorer serves, and `skill_graph_scan` embeds it into its own `vec_cmd_<dim>` table beside the skill vectors. The scan skips a row whose text has not changed and prunes a row that is no longer generated. A command row normally passes only on one of its own phrases or its slash string. It can also pass on meaning: the prompt's real embedding has to reach a cosine of 0.72 against the row and lead the next command row by 0.02. A command that passed this way ranks first only when it leads the top passing skill by the same 0.02. A prompt that asks for no change, such as \"no edits\" or \"just list\", rules out every command on meaning. A read-only question lets only a command whose route only reads pass on meaning. Without the local embedder (Ollama serving `nomic-embed-text`) no cosine exists, so command rows pass on their phrases alone.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/README.md:184:| `advisor_recommend` | Recommend skills for a prompt (`topK`, `includeAttribution`, `includeAbstainReasons`, `confidenceThreshold`, `uncertaintyThreshold`, `includeCompiledRoute`, `compiledRouteTopOnly`, `surfaceHint`) |\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/ARCHITECTURE.md:30:The recommendation command is `advisor_recommend`. The trust surface is `advisor_status`. The refresh surface is `advisor_rebuild`. The skill-graph query surface is `skill_graph_query`. The full nine-command contract, output envelope and exit taxonomy live in `references/runtime/cli-front-door-contract.md`.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/ARCHITECTURE.md:126:**Scorer.** Five lanes fuse into a single calibrated score: explicit author signals, lexical overlap, skill-graph causality, derived metadata, and a semantic shadow lane. The fusion respects per-lane weights and emits per-lane attribution alongside the final score. Command detail lives in `references/runtime/cli-front-door-contract.md`, and lane semantics live in `references/scoring/advisor-scorer.md`. Command rows get vectors of their own in a `vec_cmd_<dim>` table that the skill-graph scan fills from each row's generated `embeddingText`. The semantic lane's real cosine against those vectors is the second way a command row can pass, beside its own phrases. The pass needs a cosine of at least 0.72 and a 0.02 lead over the next command row. A no-edit prompt rules out every command on meaning, and a read-only question admits only a read-only command. With no live embedder the rule never fires.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/ARCHITECTURE.md:142:The advisor ships prompt-submit adapters for Claude, Codex, Cursor, Devin and Pi, plus the OpenCode plugin at `.opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js`. The native adapters and Pi run the same hook handler, which gates the prompt, calls the advisor CLI and renders with `renderAdvisorBrief`; the OpenCode plugin calls the CLI itself. When the handler has no brief, including after a timeout or a no-route result, it emits the directives fallback and lets the prompt proceed. The Claude adapter applies `SPECKIT_CLAUDE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS` to its advisor subprocess. When the operator leaves it unset, the Claude shim sets 2,200 ms for the hook under its own 2,500 ms kill so the fallback prints in time. Pi runs the handler in-process and races it against the budget plus 300 ms. The hook's request sends `includeCompiledRoute: true`, because the advisor derives a recommendation's slash command from the compiled route. The hook keeps that `command` field and reads nothing from the route itself. The OpenCode plugin does not send the option and gets the default of true. The plugin appends the brief to the system prompt and exposes the advisor status tool.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/ARCHITECTURE.md:152:**Test surfaces.** Default `npm test` runs unit and integration suites under `runtime/tests/`. Scorer benchmarks live under `runtime/bench/`. Operator playbook scenarios live in `manual-testing-playbook/`.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/ARCHITECTURE.md:164:| ADR-005 | Standalone MCP server boundary so routing can roll back independently | Superseded: the CLI front door (`runtime/` daemon over unix-socket IPC) is now the only surface |\n","title":"shell","metadata":{"metadata":{"status":"completed","truncated":false,"exit":0},"content":[{"type":"text","text":".skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/SKILL.md:170:- [`references/runtime/legacy-tool-bridge.md`](references/runtime/legacy-tool-bridge.md): historical bridge policy record.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/SKILL.md:173:- [`references/config/db-path-policy.md`](references/config/db-path-policy.md): package-local SQLite path policy.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/SKILL.md:220:- `sk-code` owns implementation once routing selects a code surface.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/README.md:42:system-skill-advisor is the standalone Gate 2 routing surface for Spec Kit. It runs as a resident daemon behind one CLI front door, so routing can be tuned and rolled back without touching memory or code-graph systems. It fuses five scoring lanes into one calibrated recommendation, returns per-lane attribution without leaking prompt content and surfaces a trust state on every response so the caller knows whether to use the result or treat it as degraded.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/README.md:44:The single front door is the daemon-backed CLI at `.skilled/bin/skill-advisor.cjs`, which speaks the advisor's own newline-delimited protocol over a unix socket. When the daemon is unreachable, the CLI answers from the Python scorer at `runtime/scripts/skill_advisor.py` and marks the response degraded, so the prompt-time brief reports `Advisor: stale` rather than claiming live. The skill also owns the documentation for the OpenCode plugin surface and the `/goal` plugin.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/README.md:103:- Constant advisor policy is delivered in full on the first proven message and after registered lifecycle boundaries.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/README.md:135:`advisor_recommend` accepts eight options: `topK` sets how many candidates to return (1 to 10), `includeAttribution` adds per-lane score breakdowns, `includeAbstainReasons` surfaces why lower-ranked candidates were not selected, `confidenceThreshold` overrides the minimum surfaced confidence, `uncertaintyThreshold` overrides the maximum surfaced uncertainty and `includeCompiledRoute` is an optional boolean that defaults to true. Passing `false` skips the compiled-route enrichment so no compiled-route subprocess starts. The prompt hooks send `true`, because a recommendation's `command` comes from its compiled route. `compiledRouteTopOnly` is an optional boolean that defaults to false. Passing `true` looks up the compiled route for the first recommendation only, which saves one subprocess for each further hub. The prompt hooks send it as `true`, so only the top pick can carry a `command`. `surfaceHint` forwards a code surface the caller already knows, such as `SKILLED` (1 to 64 characters), to the compiled-route front door, which ignores an unknown one. `advisor_validate` requires `confirmHeavyRun: true` because it executes the full validation bundle (corpus, holdout, parity, safety, latency).\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/README.md:176:Command rows use the same embedder. The bridge deriver writes an `embeddingText` for each of the 45 command rows the TypeScript scorer serves, and `skill_graph_scan` embeds it into its own `vec_cmd_<dim>` table beside the skill vectors. The scan skips a row whose text has not changed and prunes a row that is no longer generated. A command row normally passes only on one of its own phrases or its slash string. It can also pass on meaning: the prompt's real embedding has to reach a cosine of 0.72 against the row and lead the next command row by 0.02. A command that passed this way ranks first only when it leads the top passing skill by the same 0.02. A prompt that asks for no change, such as \"no edits\" or \"just list\", rules out every command on meaning. A read-only question lets only a command whose route only reads pass on meaning. Without the local embedder (Ollama serving `nomic-embed-text`) no cosine exists, so command rows pass on their phrases alone.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/README.md:184:| `advisor_recommend` | Recommend skills for a prompt (`topK`, `includeAttribution`, `includeAbstainReasons`, `confidenceThreshold`, `uncertaintyThreshold`, `includeCompiledRoute`, `compiledRouteTopOnly`, `surfaceHint`) |\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/ARCHITECTURE.md:30:The recommendation command is `advisor_recommend`. The trust surface is `advisor_status`. The refresh surface is `advisor_rebuild`. The skill-graph query surface is `skill_graph_query`. The full nine-command contract, output envelope and exit taxonomy live in `references/runtime/cli-front-door-contract.md`.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/ARCHITECTURE.md:126:**Scorer.** Five lanes fuse into a single calibrated score: explicit author signals, lexical overlap, skill-graph causality, derived metadata, and a semantic shadow lane. The fusion respects per-lane weights and emits per-lane attribution alongside the final score. Command detail lives in `references/runtime/cli-front-door-contract.md`, and lane semantics live in `references/scoring/advisor-scorer.md`. Command rows get vectors of their own in a `vec_cmd_<dim>` table that the skill-graph scan fills from each row's generated `embeddingText`. The semantic lane's real cosine against those vectors is the second way a command row can pass, beside its own phrases. The pass needs a cosine of at least 0.72 and a 0.02 lead over the next command row. A no-edit prompt rules out every command on meaning, and a read-only question admits only a read-only command. With no live embedder the rule never fires.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/ARCHITECTURE.md:142:The advisor ships prompt-submit adapters for Claude, Codex, Cursor, Devin and Pi, plus the OpenCode plugin at `.opencode/plugins/system-skill-advisor.js`. The native adapters and Pi run the same hook handler, which gates the prompt, calls the advisor CLI and renders with `renderAdvisorBrief`; the OpenCode plugin calls the CLI itself. When the handler has no brief, including after a timeout or a no-route result, it emits the directives fallback and lets the prompt proceed. The Claude adapter applies `SPECKIT_CLAUDE_HOOK_TIMEOUT_MS` to its advisor subprocess. When the operator leaves it unset, the Claude shim sets 2,200 ms for the hook under its own 2,500 ms kill so the fallback prints in time. Pi runs the handler in-process and races it against the budget plus 300 ms. The hook's request sends `includeCompiledRoute: true`, because the advisor derives a recommendation's slash command from the compiled route. The hook keeps that `command` field and reads nothing from the route itself. The OpenCode plugin does not send the option and gets the default of true. The plugin appends the brief to the system prompt and exposes the advisor status tool.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/ARCHITECTURE.md:152:**Test surfaces.** Default `npm test` runs unit and integration suites under `runtime/tests/`. Scorer benchmarks live under `runtime/bench/`. Operator playbook scenarios live in `manual-testing-playbook/`.\n.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/ARCHITECTURE.md:164:| ADR-005 | Standalone MCP server boundary so routing can roll back independently | Superseded: the CLI front door (`runtime/` daemon over unix-socket IPC) is now the only surface |\n"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\":\"cd /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research; grep -n \\\"attach\\\\|command row\\\\|slash command\\\\|MODE_COMMANDS\\\\|policy\\\\|surface\\\" .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/SKILL.md .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/README.md .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/ARCHITECTURE.md | head -40\"}"},"time":{"start":1794551084997,"end":1794551085642}}}}
